@chapter SCI Frontend Language

This chapter documents the SCI (Sierra Creative Interpreter) frontend language as processed by the EightBol compiler. The SCI frontend provides a Lisp-like syntax with imperative constructs for game logic programming.

@section Lexical Elements

The SCI frontend recognizes the following lexical elements:

@subsection Identifiers
Identifiers in SCI begin with a letter or underscore, followed by zero or more letters, digits, or underscores. They are used for variable names, function names, and labels.

@subsection Keywords
The following reserved keywords have special meaning in SCI:
@table @asis
@item if
@item else
@item then
@item while
@item for
@item foreach
@item break
@item continue
@item return
@item define
@item setq
@item set
@item get
@item put
@item call
@item proc
@item method
@item class
@item instance
@item send
@item super
@item self
@item print
@item format
@item strcat
@item strlen
@item substr
@item upcase
@item downcase
@item numtostr
@item strtonum
@end table

@subsection Operators
SCI supports the following operators:
@table @asis
@item + (addition)
@item - (subtraction)
@item * (multiplication)
@item / (division)
@item = (equality)
@item <> (inequality)
@item < (less than)
@item > (greater than)
@item <= (less than or equal)
@item >= (greater than or equal)
@item & (logical AND)
@item | (logical OR)
@item ! (logical NOT)
@end table

@subsection Literals
@table @asis
@item Decimal literals: Sequences of digits (0-9) representing decimal numbers (e.g., @code{123}, @code{0}).
@item Hexadecimal literals: @code{#x} prefix followed by hexadecimal digits (0-9, A-F) (e.g., @code{#xFF}, @code{#x1A2B}).
@item Octal literals: @code{#o} prefix followed by octal digits (0-7) (e.g., @code{#o377}, @code{#o755}).
@item Binary literals: @code{#b} prefix followed by binary digits (0-1) (e.g., @code{#b1010}, @code{#b11110000}).
@item Dword literals: @code{#d"} followed by a four-character ASCII string and @code{"} (e.g., @code{#d"WORD"}, @code{#d"TEST"}). The ASCII codes are combined into a 32-bit value: first character in high byte, last character in low byte.
@item String literals: Text enclosed in double quotes (e.g., @code{"Hello, world!"}).
@end table

@section Control Structures

@subsection Conditional Statements
@subsubsection if-then-else
The @code{if} statement conditionally executes code based on a boolean expression.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Condition: An expression evaluating to true (non-zero) or false (zero).
@item Then-form: One or more statements to execute if condition is true.
@item Else-form (optional): One or more statements to execute if condition is false.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
None directly; the construct affects program flow.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates the condition. If true, executes the then-form; if false and an else-form is present, executes the else-form.

@subsection Side-Effects
Depends on the executed statements; the conditional itself has no side-effects beyond flow control.

@example
(if (< score 100)
    (print "Keep going!")
    (print "You win!"))
@end example

@subsubsection while
The @code{while} loop repeatedly executes a body while a condition remains true.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Condition: Boolean expression evaluated before each iteration.
@item Body: One or more statements to execute per iteration.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
None directly.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates condition; if true, executes body and repeats; if false, exits loop.

@subsection Side-Effects
Depends on the loop body; the loop construct itself has no side-effects.

@example
(while (< attempts 3)
    (send player 'try-action)
    (setq attempts (+ attempts 1)))
@end example

@subsubsection for
The @code{for} loop iterates a variable from a start value to an end value (inclusive), incrementing by 1 each time.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Variable: Identifier to hold the current iteration value.
@item Start: Initial value for the variable.
@item End: Final value for the variable (loop stops after this value).
@item Body: One or more statements to execute per iteration.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
None directly.

@subsection Processing
Initializes variable to start value. Before each iteration, checks if variable <= end; if true, executes body and increments variable by 1; if false, exits loop.

@subsection Side-Effects
Modifies the loop variable; depends on the loop body.

@example
(for i 0 9
    (print i))
@end example

@subsubsection foreach
The @code{foreach} loop iterates over a collection (though the current SCI frontend implementation treats it similarly to @code{for} due to parser limitations; see implementation notes).

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Variable: Identifier to hold the current element.
@item Collection: Expression representing the iterable (currently not fully implemented).
@item Body: One or more statements to execute per iteration.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
None directly.

@subsection Processing
Currently parsed but not fully implemented; intended to iterate over elements of a collection.

@subsection Side-Effects
Depends on implementation.

@subsection Note
The @code{foreach} keyword is reserved but the current implementation does not provide distinct functionality from @code{for}. Future versions may extend it to handle arrays or lists.

@subsection Jump Statements
@subsubsection break
The @code{break} statement immediately exits the nearest enclosing loop (@code{while}, @code{for}, or @code{foreach}).

@subsection Inputs
None.

@subsection Outputs
None.

@subsection Processing
Transfers control to the statement following the innermost loop.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may prevent execution of loop cleanup code if present.

@example
(while t
    (if (game-over?)
        (break))
    (update-game))
@end example

@subsubsection continue
The @code{continue} statement skips the remainder of the current loop iteration and proceeds to the next iteration.

@subsection Inputs
None.

@subsection Outputs
None.

@subsection Processing
Transfers control to the loop's condition evaluation (for @code{while}) or increment step (for @code{for}/@code{foreach}).

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may skip intended loop body processing.

@example
(for i 0 9
    (if (= (mod i 2) 0)
        (continue))
    (print i)) ; Prints odd numbers 1,3,5,7,9
@end example

@subsubsection return
The @code{return} statement exits the current procedure and optionally returns a value.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Value (optional): Expression to return as the procedure result.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
The procedure returns the value of the expression (if provided) or undefined.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates the optional expression and returns from the current procedure.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; terminates procedure execution.

@example
(define (add a b)
    (return (+ a b)))
@end example

@section Procedures and Modularity

@subsubsection define
The @code{define} statement declares a new procedure.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Name: Identifier for the procedure.
@item Parameters: List of identifiers for formal parameters (currently empty in parser; see implementation).
@item Body: One or more statements comprising the procedure body.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Defines a callable procedure.

@subsection Processing
Creates a procedure object associated with the given name.

@subsection Side-Effects
Adds the procedure to the current scope; may overwrite existing definitions.

@example
(define (main)
    (print "Hello, SCI!"))
@end example

@subsubsection call
The @code{call} statement invokes a procedure by name.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Function: Identifier of the procedure to call.
@item Arguments: List of expressions to pass as parameters (currently not implemented in parser).
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns the result of the called procedure.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates arguments (if any), transfers control to the procedure, and returns the result.

@subsection Side-Effects
Depends on the called procedure.

@example
(call main)
@end example

@section Object-Oriented Features

@subsubsection class
The @code{class} statement declares a new class.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Name: Identifier for the class.
@item Body: Class definition containing variables and methods (currently not fully implemented).
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Defines a new class type.

@subsection Processing
Creates a class object in the current scope.

@subsection Side-Effects
Adds the class to the current scope; may enable instance creation.

@example
(class GameState
    ;; Variables and methods would go here
)
@end example

@subsubsection instance
The @code{instance} statement creates an instance of a class.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Class: Identifier of the class to instantiate.
@item Name: Identifier for the new instance variable.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Creates a new object of the specified class.

@subsection Processing
Allocates memory for the instance and initializes it (if applicable).

@subsection Side-Effects
Allocates memory; may invoke constructor logic.

@example
(instance GameState player1)
@end example

@subsubsection send
The @code{send} statement invokes a method on an object.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Object: Expression evaluating to an object instance.
@item Method: Identifier of the method to invoke.
@item Arguments: List of expressions to pass as parameters.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns the result of the method invocation.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates the object and arguments, locates the method in the object's class, and invokes it.

@subsection Side-Effects
Depends on the method; may modify object state.

@example
(send player 'move-to 10 20)
@end example

@subsubsection super
The @code{super} keyword refers to the parent class of the current object, used within methods to call overridden methods.

@subsection Inputs
Used as part of a method call (e.g., @code{(send super 'initialize)}).

@subsection Outputs
Refers to the parent class object.

@subsection Processing
Resolves to the parent class in the inheritance chain.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; depends on usage in method calls.

@subsubsection self
The @code{self} keyword refers to the current object instance, used within methods.

@subsection Inputs
Used as an expression representing the current object.

@subsection Outputs
The current object instance.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates to the object whose method is currently executing.

@subsection Side-Effects
None.

@example
(define (initialize)
    (send self 'set-position 0 0))
@end example

@section Built-in Functions

@subsubsection print
The @code{print} function outputs text to the console or display.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Text: String literal or expression evaluating to a string.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
None; produces side-effect of displaying text.

@subsection Processing
Converts the argument to a string (if necessary) and renders it.

@subsection Side-Effects
Updates the display or console output.

@example
(print "Score: " (numtostr score))
@end example

@subsubsection format
The @code{format} function produces formatted output similar to @code{printf}.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Format string: String with placeholders (e.g., ~d for decimal).
@item Arguments: Values to substitute into the format string.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
None; produces formatted text output.

@subsection Processing
Substitutes arguments into placeholders and outputs the result.

@subsection Side-Effects
Updates display or console.

@example
(format "Level: ~d, Lives: ~d" level lives)
@end example

@subsubsection strcat
The @code{strcat} function concatenates two or more strings.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Strings: Two or more string expressions to concatenate.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns a new string containing the concatenated result.

@subsection Processing
Combines the input strings in order.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may allocate memory for the result string.

@example
(setq greeting (strcat "Hello, " name "!"))
@end example

@subsubsection strlen
The @code{strlen} function returns the length of a string.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item String: String expression to measure.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns an integer representing the number of characters in the string.

@subsection Processing
Counts the characters in the string.

@subsection Side-Effects
None.

@example
(if (> (strlen name) 10)
    (print "Name too long!"))
@end example

@subsubsection substr
The @code{substr} function extracts a substring from a string.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item String: Source string.
@item Start: Zero-based index of the first character to extract.
@item Length: Number of characters to extract.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns a new string containing the requested substring.

@subsection Processing
Extracts characters from start index for the specified length.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may allocate memory for the result string.

@example
(setq initial (substr name 0 1))
@end example

@subsubsection upcase
The @code{upcase} function converts a string to uppercase.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item String: String expression to convert.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns a new string with all alphabetic characters converted to uppercase.

@subsection Processing
Maps each character to its uppercase equivalent.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may allocate memory for the result string.

@example
(setq shout (upcase message))
@end example

@subsubsection downcase
The @code{downcase} function converts a string to lowercase.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item String: String expression to convert.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns a new string with all alphabetic characters converted to lowercase.

@subsection Processing
Maps each character to its lowercase equivalent.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may allocate memory for the result string.

@example
(setq file-name (downcase user-input))
@end example

@subsubsection numtostr
The @code{numtostr} function converts a number to its string representation.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item Number: Numeric expression to convert.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns a string representing the number in decimal notation.

@subsection Processing
Converts the numeric value to its textual representation.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may allocate memory for the result string.

@example
(print "Value: " (numtostr counter))
@end example

@subsubsection strtonum
The @code{strtonum} function converts a string to a number.

@subsection Inputs
@itemize @bullet
@item String: String expression containing a numeric representation.
@end itemize

@subsection Outputs
Returns an integer parsed from the string; returns 0 if the string does not represent a valid number.

@subsection Processing
Parses the string as a decimal integer.

@subsection Side-Effects
None.

@example
(setq count (strtonum user-input))
@end example

@section Expressions and Operators

@subsection Arithmetic Expressions
Arithmetic expressions combine numbers using operators @code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, and @code{/}.

@subsection Inputs
Two numeric expressions.

@subsection Outputs
Returns the result of the arithmetic operation.

@subsection Processing
Performs the specified operation on the operands.

@subsection Side-Effects
None; may produce overflow or division by zero faults (see Faults section).

@example
(setq total (+ price tax))
(setq difference (- max min))
(setq product (* width height))
(setq average (/ total count))
@end example

@subsection Comparison Expressions
Comparison expressions produce boolean results based on relational operators.

@subsection Inputs
Two expressions of comparable types (typically numbers).

@subsection Outputs
Returns true (non-zero) if the comparison holds, false (zero) otherwise.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates both operands and applies the relational test.

@subsection Side-Effects
None.

@example
(if (> score high-score)
    (setq high-score score))
@end example

@subsection Logical Expressions
Logical expressions combine boolean values using operators @code{&} (AND), @code{|} (OR), and @code{!} (NOT).

@subsection Inputs
Boolean expressions.

@subsection Outputs
Returns true or false based on logical operation.

@subsection Processing
Evaluates operands and applies logical rules.

@subsection Side-Effects
None.

@example
(if (and (> health 0) (< timer 100))
    (print "Still in danger!"))
@end example

@section Faults

The SCI frontend may produce the following fault conditions:

@table @asis
@item Division by zero: Occurs when the @code{/} operator has a zero denominator.
@item Overflow: Arithmetic operations may exceed the representable range for integers.
@item Invalid memory access: Using @code{get} or @code{put} with invalid addresses.
@end table

These faults are detected at runtime and may terminate execution or invoke error handlers depending on the target platform and EightBol configuration.

@section Implementation Notes

The current SCI frontend implementation has the following limitations:
@itemize @bullet
@item The @code{foreach} construct is parsed but not distinguished from @code{for}.
@item Parameter lists in @code{define} and @code{call} are not fully implemented; procedures currently take no parameters.
@item Object-oriented features (@code{class}, @code{instance}, @code{send}) are parsed but may have limited functionality depending on the EightBol backend.
@item The @code{get} and @code{put} operators are reserved keywords but not implemented in the expression parser.
@end itemize

Future versions of the SCI frontend will address these limitations to provide full compliance with the SCI language specification.

@subsection Identifier Normalization

All identifiers in SCI source code are normalized to kebab-case (lowercase with hyphens) before AST production. This normalization:

@itemize @bullet
@item Converts uppercase letters to lowercase.
@item Replaces underscores (@code{_}) with hyphens (@code{-}).
@item Ensures consistent canonical naming across the AST.
@end itemize

Examples:
@table @asis
@item @code{MyVariableName} @tab becomes @tab @code{my-variable-name}
@item @code{my_var_name} @tab becomes @tab @code{my-var-name}
@item @code{someVar} @tab becomes @tab @code{some-var}
@item @code{CONSTANT_VALUE} @tab becomes @tab @code{constant-value}
@end table

This normalization applies to all identifiers: variables, function names, method names, class names, and object names. The backend compiler can then apply language-specific naming conventions as needed.